.TH AXPORTS 5 "2008-Feb-04" Linux "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/axports
\- AX.25 port configuration file.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
.I axports
is an ASCII file that contains information about each of the physical AX.25
ports that are to be used.
When dealing with an AX.25 utility such as
.BR call ,
it takes an argument that is the port name.
This port name is a reference to the line within
.B axports,
which has that name as its first argument.
The information on each line contains enough information to bind
the command to a particular physical AX.25 interface, this binding
is done by matching the callsign on the line in
.B axports
with the callsign of the port set by
.B kissattach.
.LP
The lines within
.I axports
must either be a comment line, which starts with a # in the first column,
or a port description in the following format, each field being delimited
by white space:
.sp
.RS
name callsign speed paclen window description
.RE
.sp
The field descriptions are:
.sp
.RS
.TP 14
.B name
is the unique identifier of the port.
This is the name given as the port argument of many of the AX.25 support
programs, and for a port served by a userspace backend it is also what
selects that backend: the name of an upstream in
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5)
or of a node in
.BR wampes.conf (5),
optionally with a
.BI : N
or
.BI : interface
suffix.  For a kernel port it is the interface name.
.TP 14
.B callsign
the callsign of the physical interface to bind to.
.TP 14
.B speed
this is the speed of interface, a value of zero means that no speed will be
set by kissattach(8).
.TP 14
.B paclen
is the default maximum packet size for this interface.
.TP 14
.B window
the default window size (aka MAXFRAME) for this interface.
.TP 14
.B description
a free format description of this interface, this field extends to the end
of the line. This field may contain spaces.
.RE
.LP
The special port name
.B loop
denotes a virtual loopback port with no physical interface behind it.
It is used together with
.BR ax25netd (8):
an AX.25 service (ax25d, axspawn, ...) that binds the callsign of the loop
port becomes reachable as a virtual station on the AGWPE loop port (255),
bridging between local clients.  The speed, paclen and window fields are
ignored for loop ports.
.PP
On systems where the AF_AX25 sockets are provided by the userspace AGWPE
shim of
.BR axsock (7)
instead of the kernel, the AGWPE port number is no longer the position of
the entry in this file: the shim resolves the
.B name
against the "port information" reply of the AGWPE server
.RB ( ax25netd ),
or, for a server on this machine, against the upstream names in
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5).
An entry whose
.B name
matches such an upstream name selects the first
channel of that upstream; a
.B :N
suffix on the
.B name
(e.g.\&
.IR radio0:1 )
selects channel N of that upstream, i.e.\& the (N+1)-th radio interface
of the upstream in question: the name before the colon picks the
upstream, the suffix the channel inside it.  This is how the channels
of one radio server (one upstream in
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5))
are addressed.  The suffix belongs to the name in the entry and is not
something a program adds at run time, so the channels are entries like any
other port:
.PP
.RS
.nf
radio0     DL9SAU-2   0  256  7   radio0, channel 0
radio0:1   DL9SAU-3   0  256  7   radio0, channel 1
radio0:2   DL9SAU-4   0  256  7   radio0, channel 2
.fi
.RE
.PP
A program is pointed at one of them by that name, as at any other port,
and channel 0 may be written out as
.I radio0:0
as well \-
the plain name is the short form of that same entry.
.PP
The
.B name
is the name of the upstream, and nothing else.  It is not the name of the
program behind it, and not the name of the device it drives: an upstream
called
.B radio0
in
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5)
that happens to be a Direwolf is still
.B radio0
here, and an entry called
.B direwolf
or
.B vhfdrop
reaches no upstream and no channel at all.  Such a name is a mistake rather
than a port of its own, and a bind that names one is refused with a note
saying so, rather than handed to the kernel \- which answers
.B EADDRNOTAVAIL
about a callsign and says nothing about the name that was wrong.  A monitor
reports
the entry as well, so a frame is named after the channel it arrived on
rather than after the radio, which is what
.BR listen (1)
prints and what
.BI "listen \-p " name :1
matches against.
.PP
Every entry has a callsign of its own because this file refuses two entries
with the same one.  That is also how an AGWPE radio server is put together:
its ports are given one SSID each, and the callsign an entry carries here is
the address that channel transmits, so it is the configuration of the radio
that has to agree with it.  The callsign is the source address of an outgoing
connection unless the program binds one itself
.RI ( "call \-s" ).
.PP
The suffix needs the server's port list, because only the server knows
which number a channel has: AGWPE numbers the channels of upstream
.I i
as
.IR i *16 + c ,
and which
.I i
an upstream has is not written down in this file.  A channel the server does
not list is refused when the socket is bound
.RB ( EADDRNOTAVAIL ),
rather than guessed at \-
a frame sent to the wrong channel cannot be taken back.  When the server is
running on the machine itself, which is the case for the default
.B 127.0.0.1:8200
and for a
.B /var/run
socket, the order of
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5)
supplies the index without a server, so a channel resolves there even
against a server that does not answer the port information query.  A
suffix above 15 is a mistake in any case: an upstream has sixteen
channels, and
.I i *16 + c
for a
.I c
of 99 would name a port of the seventh upstream.
.PP
Ports that are served by the AGWPE shim are named after the upstream they
reach, without a prefix
(e.g.\&
.IR radio0 ),
so the same name appears in this file and in
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5)
and there is nothing to keep in step but the spelling.  A prefix is not
needed to keep an AGWPE port distinguishable from a kernel interface: a name
that matches no upstream is refused at
.BR bind (2)
rather than passed on, and a name that matches one is served by it.  An
.B agwpe-
prefix is still accepted where an existing file uses one, and is stripped
before the match, so a file that has to keep a name for its kernel interface
can say
.I agwpe-radio0
to say which one it means.
.PP

Ports served by a
.B WAMPES
node are named
.BI node : interface
\-\- the part before the colon says which node, the part after it which of
that node's own AX.25 ports the traffic leaves by.  The node part is
resolved on this side and never reaches WAMPES; the interface part travels
as the port prefix WAMPES understands
.RI ( "connect hfb:DB0AAA-8" ),
the same one an operator types at an RMNC or XNET.
.PP
The node part is the name of a node in
.BR wampes.conf (5),
and any name may be given to a node there, so the "wampes" above is a
convention and not a keyword.  A node-wide entry is enough for a station with
one WAMPES: with
.PP
.RS
.nf
wampes    DL9SAU-0  9600  256  7  the node on this machine
.fi
.RE
.PP
every port of the node is reachable, and
.I wampes:hfb
then names one of them with the same callsign.  Give a
.IB name : interface
entry a callsign of its own when one specific port has to be meant every time,
because WAMPES routes by the prefix and two entries with the same callsign are
refused here.
.PP
Where the node listens is named in
.BR wampes.conf (5),
one node per line.  That file is also what makes an entry a WAMPES entry: a
port belongs to a WAMPES node when the name before the colon appears there,
so nothing is guessed from the spelling and no name is reserved.  The backend
is therefore chosen per port, at
.BR bind (2),
and no environment variable is needed;
.B AXSOCK_BACKEND
remains only as an override.
.PP
A name whose node is not in
.BR wampes.conf (5)
is refused here, before any socket is opened.
A name shaped like
.IB name : interface
is ambiguous between the two backends: a WAMPES node reads the part before
the colon as a node and the part after it as one of the node's own ports,
while
.BR axsock (7)
reads the part before it as an AGWPE upstream and the part after it as a
channel number, so the refusal says which files were asked and what each of
them answered, rather than deciding which one the name was meant for.  An
interface the node does not have is a different matter and is refused by the
node, so
.BR connect (2)
fails with ENODEV.  A name whose node is configured but which has no entry of
its own is accepted and the node's own entry is used \-\- the node then routes
the call rather than leaving by a named port, and a line on standard error
says so once.
.PP
On platforms with a native kernel AX.25 stack (Linux) the userspace
interception is not built by default and AF_AX25 sockets go to the kernel
unchanged.  If libax25 was built with it as well
.RB ( --enable-userspace-ax25 ),
the
.B AXSOCK_BACKEND
environment variable can force one backend for a whole process:
.B kernel
passes AF_AX25 and packet sockets through to the OS,
.B agwpe
routes them through the shim, and
.B wampes
hands them to a WAMPES node.  It is an override and not the normal way of
working; without it each port is decided on its own, as described above.  On
platforms without a native stack (macOS, BSD) there is no kernel to choose,
so the backend is always
.B agwpe
and AXSOCK_BACKEND is ignored.
.PP
What is left of a process-wide decision is only what a port cannot say: a
port that no backend claims, and a monitor socket, which has no port to name
at all.  Both fall back to a single probe asking whether the kernel answers
AF_AX25, and take the kernel if it does, AGWPE if it does not.
.PP
The switch itself is at
.BR bind (2),
the first moment the port is known.
.BR socket (2)
has to return a descriptor before the callsign \-
and therefore the port \-
is known, so it returns a placeholder; bind() resolves the name against this
file and hands the descriptor to whichever backend owns the port, leaving
the number the application holds untouched.  After
.BR connect (2)
it costs nothing at all: the descriptor the application holds becomes the
connection itself, so
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR poll (2)
and
.BR close (2)
reach the kernel with nothing in between.
.PP
The interception itself \-\- which calls it answers, how to put it in front of
a program that was not linked against
.B libax25
at all
.RB ( LD_PRELOAD ,
and why the macOS equivalent does not work today), what it cannot reach, and
the environment variables that steer it \-\- is described in
.BR axsock (7).
.SH FILES
.LP
@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/axports
